holds        control: the fixture points into its own species before any copy
    pairs and labeled_atoms are the reaction's own objects to begin with, so a severed reference below is the transform's doing and not the fixture's

holds        control: each transform really copies the species
    no species of any transformed reaction is an object of the original, so 'points into the copy' is a real property and not aliasing

holds        control: copy() still shares the entry by reference
    entry is the shared database object the gate reads authorship from, and copy() still hands the copy the same one -- the repair deepened nothing new

holds        control: value comparison passes whether or not the references survive
    pairs compare equal by label and labeled_atoms by key, at this tip and at the base alike. A test written that way is structurally unable to fail on this defect, which is why round 107's tests were green throughout.

note        one shared memo fixes pairs and cannot fix labeled_atoms
    deepcopy through one memo shares the atom: False
    `Molecule.__deepcopy__` (rmgpy/molecule/molecule.py:1064) is
        return self.copy(deep=True)
    -- it takes the memo and discards it, so a caller cannot make two references to one molecule come out as two references to one copy. Species-level sharing survives because Species has no such override; atom-level sharing cannot. pickle keeps its own memo, which __deepcopy__ never sees, and is 3x faster than deepcopy on the same state besides. rmgpy/molecule/ is out of gates.

note        the sites with the same defect that this round cannot touch
      reaction.py:2017   Reaction.copy          `other.pairs = deepcopy(self.pairs)` beside separately copied reactants -- the same severing, for every subclass that does not override copy()
      family.py:3854     deepcopy(rxn)          deepcopy consults Molecule.__deepcopy__, so labelled atoms are severed there too
      database.py:755    deepcopy(reaction)     the same
    
    pickle is unaffected at every one of them, which is why `copy()` now uses it.

